orangeark (1.3.0-1) focal; urgency=low

  * New: a Visio-like diagram can be drawn right in the document body
    ("插入图形"). The canvas is an anchored widget with its own toolbar:
    rectangle, rounded rectangle, ellipse, diamond (decision), parallelogram
    (data), cylinder (database), hexagon (preparation), terminator (start /
    end) and document shapes, plus connectors and arrows. Shapes are drawn by
    dragging, moved by dragging, resized with 8 handles, deleted with the
    Delete key and stacked with the popup menu.
  * New: text inside shapes, like Visio. Double click a shape (or press F2 /
    Enter) and a real text editor opens on top of it, so the input method
    works for Chinese; the text is centred and word wrapped inside the shape
    and is rendered with Pango when the canvas is drawn.
  * New: connectors snap to shapes. Dragging a line from inside one shape to
    inside another binds both ends to those shapes, so the line keeps
    following them when they are moved or resized.
  * Storage: a diagram is saved as a plain PNG image (any other CherryTree /
    OrangeArk still shows the picture) plus its vector model - in the
    "oa_drawing" attribute for XML documents, and in the image link column of
    SQLite documents behind the "oa-drawing:" marker. Undo/redo and
    copy/paste keep the diagram editable.
  * The screen colour picker now really shows the little eyedropper cursor,
    including while sampling a colour inside the document. The cursor is
    built with the Win32 cursor API from the embedded icon and re-applied
    while the mouse is captured (mouse capture also stops Windows from
    replacing it with the text view's I-beam through WM_SETCURSOR). A small
    preview of the sampled colour follows the pointer.

orangeark (1.2.4-1) focal; urgency=low

  * Ctrl/Shift multi-select works at all. This is why "多选还是不行" survived
    every earlier attempt: the click handler ended with Gtk::TreeView::
    set_cursor(), which collapses a multiple selection down to the row that
    got the keyboard focus. A GTK3 probe measured it — select() +
    set_cursor() ends with 1 row selected, select() alone keeps every picked
    row. The click handler no longer moves the focus row.
  * Dragging a multi-selection really moves every selected node now.
    node_move_after() erases the moved row, which invalidates every other
    iterator resolved before it, so nodes 2..n were silently dropped; the
    move list now holds NODE IDS and each iterator is re-resolved right
    before it is moved (same fix as node_siblings_sort).
  * Table column resize no longer targets a neighbouring column. The hit
    test combines both models of where a separator sits (the widths GTK
    really rendered + the stored widths) and takes the closest one, and
    compensates for a horizontally scrolled tree view. Measured with a
    GTK3 probe: 40/40 hits, where the old single-model code scored 1/10.
  * Table columns go back to automatic sizing (GTK's default): the fixed
    sizing tried in 1.2.3 let the last column swallow every spare pixel
    (80px stored -> 438px rendered), changing the layout basis instead of
    fixing it.

